WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.569
Monique, finally.

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:03.370
Morning.

00:00:03.436 --> 00:00:04.537
Good to see you.
[laughs]

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:05.338
How are you?

00:00:05.405 --> 00:00:06.806
Good to see you, love.

00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:07.607
You good?

00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:08.408
I'm great.

00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:09.476
What an amazing place.

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:10.310
Welcome to New Zealand.

00:00:10.377 --> 00:00:12.278
[music playing]

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:13.913
GORDON RAMSAY
(VOICEOVER): Monique Fiso

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:17.550
is one of New Zealand's top
chefs, trained in Michelin

00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:22.222
star restaurants but passionate
about her Maori heritage.

00:00:22.288 --> 00:00:27.327
She's on a one-woman mission to
blend the food of her ancestors

00:00:27.394 --> 00:00:30.597
with the world of fine dining.

00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:34.234
Now, never ever ventured
into that Maori cuisine.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.269
So what do I need to understand?

00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:37.871
There's four main ecosystems.

00:00:37.937 --> 00:00:38.972
There's the oceans
and the river.

00:00:39.038 --> 00:00:41.274
That's Tangaroa, god of the sea.

00:00:41.341 --> 00:00:44.411
And then there's the
mountains and the forest--

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:45.912
Papatuanuku, earth mother.

00:00:45.979 --> 00:00:46.713
Papa what?

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:47.747
Papatuanuku.

00:00:47.814 --> 00:00:49.082
OK, Papatuanuku.

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:50.850
Papatuanuku.

00:00:50.917 --> 00:00:51.851
Papatuanuku.

00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:52.652
That's the one.

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:54.220
It's a complex cuisine.

00:00:54.287 --> 00:00:56.189
There's a lot of
customs involved.

00:00:56.256 --> 00:00:59.292
It's not just about growing
food and just making it.

00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:01.127
There's a lot of
different traditions

00:01:01.194 --> 00:01:03.430
that are interwoven
in all of the food.

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:06.599
[music playing]

00:01:06.666 --> 00:01:08.268
GORDON RAMSAY (VOICEOVER):
The Maori first arrived

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:10.537
in New Zealand on
pioneering voyages

00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:13.840
from Polynesia in
the 13th century.

00:01:13.907 --> 00:01:16.443
Although they brought some crops
with them, much of their diet

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:20.413
relied on hunting and gathering
from the forest and ocean,

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.649
a tradition which Maori
chefs like Monique

00:01:22.715 --> 00:01:26.486
are keeping alive
through their cooking.

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:29.522
Now, foraging, is it
big on the menu here?

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:31.691
It is big on the menu here.

00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:33.827
A lot of the time, it's the
only way to get your hands

00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:34.661
on these ingredients.

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:35.995
Wow.

00:01:36.062 --> 00:01:37.797
And as you can see, I've
got two machetes, one of which

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:38.832
is for you.

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:39.866
That's my machete?

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:41.134
MONIQUE FISO:
That's your machete.

00:01:41.201 --> 00:01:42.535
[laughs]

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:44.971
Ask all the chefs
at my restaurant.

00:01:45.038 --> 00:01:46.339
We all own a machete each.

00:01:46.406 --> 00:01:48.174
I know, but you don't walk
home at night with this thing,

00:01:48.241 --> 00:01:49.108
do you?

00:01:49.175 --> 00:01:50.243
No, we tuck it
in our backpacks.

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:52.145
You tuck it in your backpack.

00:01:52.212 --> 00:01:53.146
These machetes are lethal.

00:01:53.213 --> 00:01:54.747
[music playing]

00:01:54.814 --> 00:01:55.882
GORDON RAMSAY
(VOICEOVER): Monique

00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:58.218
is a woman I'm
definitely not going

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:00.720
to argue with,
especially when she's

00:02:00.787 --> 00:02:03.289
on the hunt for wild food.

00:02:03.356 --> 00:02:07.193
So this is not like walking
down a supermarket aisle.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:09.429
No, this is one
of the hard work

00:02:09.496 --> 00:02:11.297
we do to get the
food we need to eat.

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:15.301
They're everywhere.

00:02:15.368 --> 00:02:16.202
Monique, really?

00:02:16.269 --> 00:02:19.439
Yeah, just keep going chef.

00:02:19.506 --> 00:02:21.274
[bleep]

00:02:21.341 --> 00:02:22.809
GORDON RAMSAY (VOICEOVER):
But these vines are

00:02:22.876 --> 00:02:24.477
more than just a jungle gym.

00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:28.114
At their tip is a
secret Maori delicacy.

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:29.983
So these are the young
shoots that come off the vines,

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:31.417
and they're super tender.

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:32.852
Wow, you can eat that?

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:33.887
MONIQUE FISO: You can eat that.

00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:36.122
They're hard to find.

00:02:36.189 --> 00:02:37.323
It's like bush asparagus.

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:40.126
Give it a try.

00:02:40.193 --> 00:02:40.994
They're really good.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:41.794
They are good.

00:02:41.861 --> 00:02:42.629
They are like asparagus.

00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:43.429
Mm.

00:02:43.496 --> 00:02:44.564
So soft and delicate.

00:02:44.631 --> 00:02:46.199
Yeah.

00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:48.434
Delicious.

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:49.769
GORDON RAMSAY
(VOICEOVER): All I can

00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:52.672
see in this tangled forest
are potential personal injury

00:02:52.739 --> 00:02:53.773
claims.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:55.208
Oh, here's a good one.

00:02:55.275 --> 00:02:56.876
GORDON RAMSAY (VOICEOVER):
But for machete Monique,

00:02:56.943 --> 00:02:59.178
it's like an all
you can eat buffet.

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:00.013
What's that?

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:00.747
Horopito.

00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:01.814
Horopito.

00:03:01.881 --> 00:03:03.550
Yes, it's a native
bush pepper tree.

00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:04.684
Wow.

00:03:04.751 --> 00:03:06.219
MONIQUE FISO: It's super
spicy, super peppery.

00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:09.489
Have a munch on that.

00:03:09.556 --> 00:03:10.790
Wow.

00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:12.225
Boy, that one's spicy, whew.

00:03:12.292 --> 00:03:13.192
GORDON RAMSAY: That is spicy.

00:03:13.259 --> 00:03:15.295
That's incredible from that.

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:16.563
Quite numb now, my tongue.
- Yeah.

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:17.697
From hot--

00:03:17.764 --> 00:03:18.898
And it's going to stay
that way for a while.

00:03:18.965 --> 00:03:19.699
Is it?

00:03:19.766 --> 00:03:20.633
[laughter]

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.502
[music playing]

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:24.437
GORDON RAMSAY (VOICEOVER):
There's now a full-scale forest

00:03:24.504 --> 00:03:25.805
fire in my mouth.

00:03:25.872 --> 00:03:28.074
But Monique's not done yet.

00:03:28.141 --> 00:03:29.142
It's amazing.

00:03:29.208 --> 00:03:30.944
From the beach, it
doesn't look that steep.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:32.946
GORDON RAMSAY (VOICEOVER): She's
got another surprise for me

00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:36.182
high up in the forest canopy.

00:03:36.249 --> 00:03:37.684
That is deep in
there, Monique.

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:38.484
I know.

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:39.619
It's a good workout.

00:03:39.686 --> 00:03:40.720
Isn't it?

00:03:40.787 --> 00:03:43.823
Next challenge, this
is a fuchsia tree.

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.091
And the best thing
about this tree--

00:03:45.158 --> 00:03:46.259
- Go on.
- --the berries.

00:03:46.326 --> 00:03:49.996
And they're usually way up high.

00:03:50.063 --> 00:03:51.898
So the best ones,
they're bright purple,

00:03:51.965 --> 00:03:52.699
and they're super sweet.

00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:53.800
Yeah.

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:55.134
But they're just a
bit of a pain to get.

00:03:55.201 --> 00:03:56.369
So--

00:03:56.436 --> 00:03:57.370
So we just shake the
tree, and they'll drop down?

00:03:57.437 --> 00:03:58.538
No, you're going
to have to go up.

00:03:58.605 --> 00:03:59.339
Seriously?

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:00.473
MONIQUE FISO: Yeah.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:01.774
You sure they'll take
my weight, the branches?

00:04:01.841 --> 00:04:02.909
MONIQUE FISO: We'll
soon find out.

00:04:02.976 --> 00:04:06.813
[music playing]

00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:13.886
I'll wait here.

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:14.787
That's very kind of you.

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:23.863
Honestly, you said
come foraging, right?

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:27.367
MONIQUE FISO: Yeah, I didn't say
it would all be on the ground.

00:04:27.433 --> 00:04:29.268
Just a little bit further now.

00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:30.069
[cracking]

00:04:30.136 --> 00:04:31.371
Oh, [bleep].

00:04:31.437 --> 00:04:32.305
Did you hear that?

00:04:32.372 --> 00:04:34.307
I heard that.

00:04:34.374 --> 00:04:36.776
Keep going.

00:04:36.843 --> 00:04:40.546
You're like Tarzan up there.

00:04:40.613 --> 00:04:41.547
Can you see them?

00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:43.916
I can see them
right in front of you.

00:04:43.983 --> 00:04:44.717
Right.

00:04:44.784 --> 00:04:47.954
[music playing]

00:04:50.023 --> 00:04:50.823
Bloody hell, it's windy.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:55.728
So the bright
purple ones, right?

00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:58.698
MONIQUE FISO: Bright purple,
those are the ones we're after.

00:04:58.765 --> 00:05:00.333
Can you toss them down?

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:01.300
They look like tiny dates.

00:05:04.437 --> 00:05:05.171
Wow.

00:05:05.238 --> 00:05:06.472
How good are those?

00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:09.342
GORDON RAMSAY: It's almost
like a treetop dessert.

00:05:09.409 --> 00:05:11.778
And I just submit they're
one of the best ingredients

00:05:11.844 --> 00:05:13.312
that we have in this country.

00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:15.615
GORDON RAMSAY: They're bloody
tasty, fuchsia berries.

00:05:15.682 --> 00:05:16.382
Fuchsia berries.

00:05:19.185 --> 00:05:21.154
GORDON RAMSAY: Pretty hairy
scaling that tree, especially

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:23.956
the wind, but
another hidden gem,

00:05:24.023 --> 00:05:25.391
and what a delicious
little berry.

00:05:25.458 --> 00:05:26.259
There's no seeds inside.

00:05:26.325 --> 00:05:27.226
So that's the nice thing.

00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:29.429
So it's just
bursting with flavor.

00:05:29.495 --> 00:05:30.830
The pain in the ass
was they're at the top

00:05:30.897 --> 00:05:31.764
of the tree, not the bottom.

00:05:31.831 --> 00:05:35.001
[music playing]

